WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:05.290
It's Tuesday, October 27th, 2015, a significant
day in YouTube history because tomorrow, YouTube

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.890
Red launches, which may or may not change
online video forever.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.360
Also, you'll notice that I trimmed my beard
because Nerdfighters had started to refer

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.650
to it as the Under Puff.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.650
Right, but YouTube Red.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.760
So until now, YouTube has always been like
network television and radio before it - an

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.660
advertising-supported medium.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.420
Now there are obvious advantages to this.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.699
Creators get paid unlike most social media
sites, and also, it's free for viewers.

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:28.670
But there are also disadvantages.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.199
Ad-supported media tends to seek the broadest
possible audience because what advertisers

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:36.019
really want is the most possible eyeballs
to see their message.

00:00:36.019 --> 00:00:39.699
And with ad-supported media, we generally
end up with stuff that lots of people will

00:00:39.699 --> 00:00:43.710
watch over stuff that, like, some people will
really, really love.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.960
You know like broad spectrum shows like Law
&amp; Order can succeed, but niche shows like

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.129
Firefly or Arrested Development can't.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:52.699
Also, I know you probably don't think you're
affected by advertising, but you are.

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:56.589
To quote Emily Nussbaum's brilliant recent
essay on what advertising has done to television,

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:01.269
"Our comfort with being sold to may look like
savvy, but it feels like innocence."

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:04.370
I mean, Hank, corporations know a lot more
about what influences my consumptive habits

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:05.370
than I do.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.270
That phenomenon has been studied extensively,
sources in the dooblydoo, where you can also

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.610
find a link to Nussbaum's essay.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.200
So OK, having acknowledged my advertising
ambivalence, let's explore my YouTube Red

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.200
ambivalence.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.310
Here's the idea - for ten dollars a month,
you get ad-free YouTube, off-line watching,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.620
and all the music you want.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.719
So it's like Spotify plus ad-free YouTube.

00:01:21.719 --> 00:01:25.390
Also, there will be some new original shows
from people like PewDiePie and other YouTubers

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.590
that are only available to YouTube Red subscribers.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:31.149
So Hank, the question I've heard the most
from people is "Is this good for creators?"

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:33.920
which speaks a lot to how awesome the YouTube
viewership is.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.180
The answer to that question is... yeah?

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.100
Some portion of that ten dollars per month
will go to the YouTubers you watch, I don't

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.100
really know how much yet.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.619
And per viewer, YouTube Red will generate
more money for creators than advertising.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:47.469
That said, it's like a thousand times more
valuable to support a creator through Patreon

00:01:47.469 --> 00:01:50.810
or through buying merch than through either
YouTube Red or advertising.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.270
Actually, it's not like a thousand, it is
a thousand times better.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.270
Maybe even two thousand.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.030
The best way to financially support creators
you care about remains directly.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.600
So I don't think that's the reason to get
YouTube Red.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.790
But I do think there are good reasons.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:02.790
I mean I'm gonna get it.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.259
But anyway Hank, here's what concerns me the
most: will YouTube Red create two classes

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.650
of YouTube viewers - those who can pay and
those who can't?

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:12.240
One of the things that makes YouTube really
special is that at it's best, it's a flat

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.040
platform for both creators and audiences,
right?

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.940
Like I have the same capacity to reach an
audience sitting here in my basement as a

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.410
major media company does.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.150
And on the audience side, if you have access
to a device and the internet, all of YouTube

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.150
is available to you.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.080
Now that started to change a little on the
creator side of things as production values

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.550
have risen, but I really don't want it to
change on the audience side.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.540
Like YouTube is gonna remain essentially the
same for everyone who doesn't become a Red

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:35.540
subscriber.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.630
The only difference is that you won't have
access to these new high-budget original shows.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.070
Like PewDiePie is making a horror show with
the producers of The Walking Dead, and that

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:44.070
sounds awesome!

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.880
And it would also be completely impossible
in an advertising-supported medium.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.740
But Nerdfighters, I wonder if you would be
excited or scared if your favorite creators

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:52.740
did such a show.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:53.980
I'm asking for a friend by the way.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:57.510
Like, it allows creators to have the kinds
of budgets you can't have for advertising-supported

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.460
projects, but on the other hand, it limits
the number of people who can actually see

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:02.970
the thing to Red subscribers.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.380
In short Hank, I don't know how to feel about
all this stuff.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.200
We've always been uncomfortable with advertising,
and it's nice to see YouTube moving away from

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.650
sole reliance upon it.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.170
But I think YouTube Red will be a good option
for a lot of people without harming the experience

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.530
of the majority who stick with ad-supported
YouTube.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.920
And in general, I think that subscriptions
make viewers more powerful and brands slightly

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:20.920
less so.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:21.920
And that's good news.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.280
But I'm not sure I want YouTube to look like
cable TV when it grows up.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.230
I want YouTube to be driven by the stuff that
makes it interesting, by passionate fan communities

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.220
who do awesome stuff together by the direct
relationship between creator and audience.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.980
I mean, at it's best, YouTube isn't something
you watch; it's something you're part of.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.770
So the question becomes, is YouTube Red a
step toward that promise land or away from

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:40.770
it?

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:41.770
I don't know.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:42.770
That's not a rhetorical question.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.010
I look forward to continuing the conversation
in comments, Nerdfighters.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:46.300
Hank, I'll see you tomorrow.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.300
Nope, Friday.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.409
Gosh, I was so in Brotherhood 2.0 mode.

